      <p class="kicker">Lagom</p>
      <h1>Decisions, including the open one.</h1>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A choice that is hard to undo is written as an ADR: the problem, the alternatives, the
          decision, and how to reverse it. The layout of the repository is one of them (ADR-0006):
          shared backbone, one directory per source, and what a person sees under products. Adding
          a source is supposed to be additive.
        </p>
        <p>
          Three environments share the one node (ADR-0007): dev against staging's data, plus
          staging and prod namespaces. The accepted storage decision (ADR-0008) keeps every layer
          in Postgres until a lake has a measured reason. The alternative, a lakehouse from the
          first source, was rejected because the volume did not need it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The portfolio decision (ADR-0022) says each architecture is a showcase on that backbone,
          published as static pages, with no login for public data. A later decision (ADR-0023)
          proposes the SME stack: DuckDB, DuckLake, a Postgres catalog, files on Cloudflare R2. It
          is still proposed. The spike that would accept or revise it is open. The job that runs
          already loads DuckLake, which the card below states without closing the decision.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figures page="decisions" />
